course: dictionary
lesson: 4891
title: Ignorance: not knowing yet
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ignorance means not knowing about something yet. It is a word for a gap in what you know, and everyone has gaps. You are ignorant of many things, like how a volcano works or how to speak Japanese, and that is fine. Learning fills the gap.
explain: Read the words around the word to find its meaning. «The Apostrophe Protection Society was set up in 2001 and closed in 2019; its founder John Richards said "ignorance and laziness" had won.» The word next to laziness is a bad thing, so ignorance here is probably not a good thing either. It means not knowing, and in this sentence it is linked with people not caring enough to learn.
explain: Ignorance can also mean rude or unfriendly behaviour that comes from not knowing or not caring how others feel. Some phrases help too. «Ignorance is bliss» means it can seem happier not to know something, and «out of ignorance» means because you did not know.
fact: Ignorance means not knowing about something; a lack of knowledge or information.
fact: Everyone is ignorant of some things, and learning cures ignorance.
fact: Ignorance can also mean rude or unfriendly behaviour that comes from not caring how others feel.
fact: «Ignorance is bliss» means it can be happier not to know something.
fact: «Out of ignorance» means because you did not know, and «in ignorance of» means without knowing about something.
exercise: In «ignorance and laziness» from the sentence about the Apostrophe Protection Society, what does ignorance mean? | *Not knowing about something | Kind help to others | A bright light in the dark | Being very clever at spelling
exercise: Ben says, "Ignorance is bliss, so I will never read the news." What does the saying mean? | Knowing everything makes you sad | *It can seem happier not to know | Ben must be very kind | Reading the news is always the best choice
exercise: Which phrase means "because you did not know"? | Once in a while | In a hurry | *Out of ignorance | By mistake on purpose
test: Sam says ignorance is always rude. Is he right? | No, ignorance means being very clever | Yes, ignorance is a kind of lunch | *No, ignorance can just mean not knowing yet, and learning cures it | Yes, ignorance is always rude
test: Which sentence shows ignorance in the rude sense? | He put his hand up to ask what a word meant | She asked her teacher to explain the rules again | *He laughed at the new boy's accent, showing no care for how it made him feel | He read a book about the stars
exam: Read: «Ignorance of the law is no excuse.» What does this suggest? | Laws are only written in big books | *Not knowing a rule does not excuse breaking it | Ignorance is always a good excuse | Everyone must learn every law by heart
